. 
. * H2: Having daughters leads to more left-leaning political preferences. *
. * That is, denoting Y as a measure of left-leaning political preferences, *
. * (Y for respondents with daughters) > (Y for respondents without daughters).
>   *
. * “Left-leaning political preferences”    *
. 
. * Dependent variable *
. * Left-right slider has a long tradition in political science (e.g. Inglehart
> , 2018 --> Inglehart, R. (2018). Culture shift in advanced industrial society
> . Princeton University Press. *
. * Party-based dummies are standard in the economics of voting. Congleton, R. 
> D., Grofman, B. N., & Voigt, S. (Eds.). (2018). The Oxford Handbook of Public
>  Choice, Volume 2. Oxford University Press. *
. * Which party is that?” – categorical code for German parties. https://paneld
> ata.org/soep-core/datasets/pl/plh0012_v6 *
. * Party closeness dummy – 1 = SPD, Greens, Die Linke; 0 = CDU/CSU, FDP, AfD, 
> none/other. *
. 
. gen party_closeness_dummy = .
(663,123 missing values generated)

. replace party_closeness_dummy = 1 if inlist(plh0012_v6, 1, 5, 6, 17)
(104,856 real changes made)

. replace party_closeness_dummy = . if inlist(plh0012_v6, -5, -4, -2, -1)
(0 real changes made)

. replace party_closeness_dummy = 0 if inlist(plh0012_v6, 2, 3, 4, 8, 27, 30, 3
> 1, 0)
(93,525 real changes made)

. 
. replace party_closeness_dummy = 1 if inlist(plh0012_v6, -1, -2)
(278,514 real changes made)

